CH.append({
    "title": "Memory management",
    "maps": [{"title": "Managing main memory", "links": [
        "Memory manager | tracks | Every memory location",
        "Logical address | is mapped by | MMU",
        "MMU | produces | Physical address",
        "Address binding | happens at | Compile, load or execution time",
        "Swapping | moves process to | Disk",
        "Fragmentation | has two kinds | Internal and external",
        "Compaction | reduces | External fragmentation",
        "Paging | divides memory into | Frames",
        "Paging | uses | Page table",
        "Segmentation | uses | Segment table",
        "Virtual memory [ch10] | builds on | Paging"]}],
    "summary": [
        "Memory management handles primary memory and moves processes between main memory and disk. It tracks every memory location, decides which process gets memory and when, and updates the status when memory is freed.",
        "Programs use logical addresses; the memory management unit translates them to physical addresses at run time. Memory can be allocated in single or multiple partitions, which leads to fragmentation: external, when free space is split into unusable pieces, and internal, when a block is larger than needed.",
        "Paging splits a process into fixed-size pages loaded into fixed-size frames, avoiding external fragmentation. Segmentation splits a process into variable-size segments that match program modules."],
    "glossary": [
        {"term": "Logical address", "def": "An address generated by a program; the set of them is the logical address space."},
        {"term": "Physical address", "def": "A location in main memory; the set of them is the physical address space."},
        {"term": "MMU", "def": "The memory management unit, hardware that maps virtual addresses to physical addresses at run time."},
        {"term": "Swapping", "def": "Moving a process temporarily out of main memory to disk and back later."},
        {"term": "Relocation and limit registers", "def": "Hardware that adds a base to each logical address and checks it against a range."},
        {"term": "External fragmentation", "def": "Enough total free memory exists, but not contiguously."},
        {"term": "Internal fragmentation", "def": "A block assigned to a process is larger than needed; the rest is wasted."},
        {"term": "Compaction", "def": "Shuffling memory to put all free space in one block. Needs dynamic relocation."},
        {"term": "Page and frame", "def": "A fixed-size block of a process, and the same-size block of physical memory that holds it."},
        {"term": "Page table", "def": "Maps each page of a process to a frame in physical memory."},
        {"term": "Segment", "def": "A variable-size piece of a program, such as its main function or a data structure."}],
    "keyPoints": [
        "Symbolic addresses become relative at compile time and physical at load time.",
        "Compile-time and load-time binding give identical virtual and physical addresses; execution-time binding does not.",
        "The MMU translates logical to physical addresses at run time.",
        "Static linking combines modules into one program; dynamic linking keeps a reference resolved at run time.",
        "Fragmentation: external (enough space, not contiguous) and internal (block bigger than needed).",
        "Paging: pages and frames same size, page table, offset stays the same. Segmentation: variable-size segments with base and length."],
    "lesson": [
        T("Memory management is the function of an operating system that handles primary memory and moves processes back and forth between main memory and disk during execution. It keeps track of every memory location, whether allocated or free. It checks how much memory to allocate, decides which process gets memory and when, and updates the status when memory is freed."),
        T("The process address space is the set of logical addresses a process references in its code. With 32-bit addressing, addresses range from 0 to 0x7fffffff, which is 2 to the 31st power possible numbers, a theoretical size of 2 gigabytes. The OS maps logical addresses to physical addresses when memory is allocated."),
        T("There are three kinds of addresses. Symbolic addresses are used in source code: variable names, constants and instruction labels. A compiler converts them into relative addresses. The loader generates the physical addresses when the program is loaded into main memory."),
        T("Virtual and physical addresses are the same under compile-time and load-time address binding. They differ under execution-time binding. The set of all logical addresses is the logical address space; the set of corresponding physical addresses is the physical address space."),
        T("The run-time mapping from virtual to physical addresses is done by the memory management unit, the MMU, a hardware device."),
        T("Next, loading and linking. With static loading the whole program is loaded into memory before it starts. With dynamic loading, routines are stored on disk in relocatable form and loaded only when the program needs them. Static linking combines all needed modules into one executable to avoid run-time dependency."),
        T("With dynamic linking the program only holds a reference to the module and the rest is resolved at run time. Dynamic link libraries, DLLs, on Windows and shared objects on Unix are examples."),
        T("Swapping is a mechanism where a process is swapped temporarily out of main memory to secondary storage, making memory available to others. Later the system swaps it back. Swapping affects performance, but it lets the system run multiple and big processes in parallel, which is why it is also described as a technique for memory compaction."),
        T("The total swap time includes moving the process to disk, copying it back, and the time to regain memory. Here is a worked example, with a correction. A process of 2048 KB on a disk transferring about 1 MB per second: 2048 KB divided by 1024 KB per second is 2 seconds, one way. The tutorial writes this as 200 milliseconds, which is a slip; 2 seconds is 2000 milliseconds. Swapping in and out takes about 4 seconds, plus overhead."),
        T("Memory allocation. Main memory has two partitions: low memory, where the operating system resides, and high memory, where user processes are held. In single-partition allocation a relocation register holds the smallest physical address and a limit register holds the range of logical addresses. Every logical address must be less than the limit register. This protects processes from each other and from changing OS code and data."),
        T("In multiple-partition allocation, main memory is divided into a number of fixed-size partitions, each holding one process. When a partition is free, a process is selected from the input queue and loaded into it. When the process terminates, the partition becomes available again."),
        T("Fragmentation. As processes are loaded and removed, free memory is broken into little pieces. Eventually processes cannot be placed in blocks that are too small. There are two kinds. External fragmentation: total free space is enough, but it is not contiguous. Internal fragmentation: the block assigned is bigger than needed, and the unused part cannot be used by another process."),
        T("External fragmentation can be reduced by compaction, shuffling memory contents so free memory forms one large block. To make compaction possible, relocation must be dynamic. Internal fragmentation can be reduced by assigning the smallest partition that is still large enough for the process."),
        T("Paging. Paging breaks the process address space into blocks of the same size called pages, with sizes a power of 2, between 512 and 8192 bytes. Main memory is divided into fixed-size frames, the same size as pages, so there is no external fragmentation. A page-map table records which frame holds each page."),
        T("Address translation. A logical address is a page number plus a page offset. A physical address is a frame number plus the same offset. The system gives the page a frame and records it in the page table. When memory runs out, the OS moves idle pages to secondary memory and brings them back when needed."),
        M(r"\text{physical address} = \text{frame}(p) \times \text{page size} + d \qquad \text{where } p = \lfloor a / \text{page size} \rfloor,\; d = a \bmod \text{page size}"),
        T("Paging reduces external fragmentation but still has internal fragmentation, because the last page may be partly empty. It is simple and efficient, and swapping is easy because pages and frames are equal in size. The page table needs extra memory, which hurts small-RAM systems."),
        T("Segmentation divides each job into segments of different sizes, one for each module of related functions. Each segment is its own logical address space. Segments are loaded into non-contiguous memory, but each segment sits in a contiguous block. It works like paging, but segments have variable length, while pages are fixed.",
          "The OS keeps a segment map table per process, storing for each segment its starting address and length. A reference gives a segment number and an offset."),
        T("The practice section has you implement the relocation and limit check, the three classic placement strategies, page-table translation and the swap-time calculation.")],
    "quiz": [
        {"q": "What does the MMU do?", "options": ["Compiles programs", "Maps virtual addresses to physical addresses at run time", "Stores files", "Prints pages"], "answer": 1, "explain": "It is a hardware device."},
        {"q": "External fragmentation means what?", "options": ["A block is larger than needed", "Enough total free memory but not contiguous", "A page is too small", "A segment is empty"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Compaction can fix it."},
        {"q": "How does paging avoid external fragmentation?", "options": ["It deletes processes", "Pages and frames are the same fixed size", "It uses a printer", "It increases the clock"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Any free frame fits any page."},
        {"q": "How long does swapping out and in a 2048 KB process take at 1 MB per second (transfer only)?", "options": ["About 400 ms", "About 4 seconds", "About 40 seconds", "About 2 ms"], "answer": 1, "explain": "2 seconds each way: 2048 divided by 1024 is 2 s, so 4 s total."}],
    "cards": [
        {"q": "Logical address in paging?", "a": "Page number plus page offset."},
        {"q": "Physical address in paging?", "a": "Frame number plus the same offset."},
        {"q": "Internal fragmentation fix?", "a": "Assign the smallest partition large enough for the process."},
        {"q": "Paging vs segmentation?", "a": "Paging uses fixed-size pages; segmentation uses variable-size segments."}],
    "practice": [
        {"type": "python", "title": "Relocation and limit", "intro": "A single-partition system adds a relocation register (base) to every logical address, but each logical address must be less than the limit register. Write translate(addr, base, limit): return the physical address, or -1 if the address is outside the limit (a protection fault).", "fn": "translate",
         "starter": "def translate(addr, base, limit):\n    # your code\n    pass\n",
         "tests": [{"args": [299, 1000, 300], "expect": 1299}, {"args": [300, 1000, 300], "expect": -1}, {"args": [0, 14000, 3000], "expect": 14000}, {"args": [-1, 1000, 300], "expect": -1}],
         "hint": "if addr < 0 or addr >= limit return -1, otherwise base + addr.",
         "solution": "def translate(addr, base, limit):\n    if addr < 0 or addr >= limit:\n        return -1\n    return base + addr\n"},
        {"type": "python", "title": "Placement strategies", "intro": "Free holes are a list of sizes. Write allocate(holes, size, strategy). strategy is \"first\" (the first hole big enough), \"best\" (the smallest hole big enough) or \"worst\" (the largest hole). Return the index of the chosen hole, or -1 if none is large enough. For ties, take the lowest index.", "fn": "allocate",
         "starter": "def allocate(holes, size, strategy):\n    # your code\n    pass\n",
         "tests": [{"args": [[100, 500, 200, 300, 600], 212, "first"], "expect": 1}, {"args": [[100, 500, 200, 300, 600], 212, "best"], "expect": 3}, {"args": [[100, 500, 200, 300, 600], 212, "worst"], "expect": 4}, {"args": [[100, 500, 200, 300, 600], 700, "best"], "expect": -1}, {"args": [[50, 50], 50, "best"], "expect": 0}],
         "hint": "Build the list of indices whose hole >= size. Pick by strategy.",
         "solution": "def allocate(holes, size, strategy):\n    fit = [i for i in range(len(holes)) if holes[i] >= size]\n    if not fit:\n        return -1\n    if strategy == 'first':\n        return fit[0]\n    if strategy == 'best':\n        return min(fit, key=lambda i: (holes[i], i))\n    return min(fit, key=lambda i: (-holes[i], i))\n"},
        {"type": "python", "title": "Page table lookup", "intro": "Write page_translate(addr, page_size, table). table[p] is the frame number of page p, or -1 if the page is not in memory. Return the physical address, or -1 if the page number is outside the table or not in memory.", "fn": "page_translate",
         "starter": "def page_translate(addr, page_size, table):\n    # your code\n    pass\n",
         "tests": [{"args": [123, 100, [5, 2, 7]], "expect": 223}, {"args": [250, 100, [5, 2, 7]], "expect": 750}, {"args": [350, 100, [5, 2, 7]], "expect": -1}, {"args": [50, 100, [-1, 2]], "expect": -1}, {"args": [4100, 4096, [3, 9]], "expect": 36868}],
         "hint": "p = addr // page_size, d = addr % page_size, then frame * page_size + d.",
         "solution": "def page_translate(addr, page_size, table):\n    p = addr // page_size\n    d = addr % page_size\n    if p >= len(table) or table[p] < 0:\n        return -1\n    return table[p] * page_size + d\n"},
        {"type": "python", "title": "Swap time", "intro": "Write swap_time_ms(size_kb, rate_kb_per_s): the transfer time in milliseconds to swap a process out and back in (two transfers). Overhead is ignored.", "fn": "swap_time_ms",
         "starter": "def swap_time_ms(size_kb, rate_kb_per_s):\n    # your code\n    pass\n",
         "tests": [{"args": [2048, 1024], "expect": 4000, "approx": 0.001}, {"args": [1000, 1000], "expect": 2000, "approx": 0.001}, {"args": [512, 2048], "expect": 500, "approx": 0.001}],
         "hint": "One way is size / rate seconds. Multiply by 2 and by 1000.",
         "solution": "def swap_time_ms(size_kb, rate_kb_per_s):\n    return 2 * size_kb / rate_kb_per_s * 1000\n"},
        {"type": "match", "title": "Memory terms", "intro": "Match each term with its meaning.", "pairs": [["Internal fragmentation", "Block assigned is bigger than needed"], ["External fragmentation", "Free space is enough but not contiguous"], ["Compaction", "Shuffle memory to make one large free block"], ["Frame", "Fixed-size block of physical memory"], ["Segment", "Variable-size logical piece of a program"]]}]
})

